1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,869
September, nineteen forty.

2
00:00:01,869 --> 00:00:05,231
German bombers come back to
London every single night.

3
00:00:05,231 --> 00:00:09,316
And every evening, thousands of
families walk down into the Tube,

4
00:00:09,316 --> 00:00:12,081
carrying blankets, bread
and their children.

5
00:00:12,081 --> 00:00:13,886
The government had banned it.

6
00:00:13,886 --> 00:00:18,930
So how do you eat, sleep and survive under
a burning city for fifty-seven nights?

7
00:00:19,630 --> 00:00:23,641
In nineteen forty, London already
had the perfect bomb shelter:

8
00:00:23,641 --> 00:00:26,698
miles of deep railway
tunnels under its streets.

9
00:00:26,698 --> 00:00:30,610
Some platforms sat more than
twenty metres below the pavement.

10
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:35,242
But the government said no. The
Tube had to keep moving workers,

11
00:00:35,242 --> 00:00:38,806
soldiers and supplies, so the
platforms had to stay clear.

12
00:00:38,806 --> 00:00:41,518
Officials also feared
that frightened people

13
00:00:41,518 --> 00:00:44,230
would go down and simply
never come back up.

14
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:48,927
So Londoners found a loophole, and
it cost them one and a half pence.

15
00:00:48,927 --> 00:00:53,734
They bought the cheapest ticket, walked
down to the platform, and refused to leave.

16
00:00:53,734 --> 00:00:58,600
Station staff could not throw out thousands
of paying passengers, night after night.

17
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:02,393
In the first nights of the
Blitz, crowds at Liverpool

18
00:01:02,393 --> 00:01:05,604
Street station pushed in as
soon as the sirens started.

19
00:01:05,604 --> 00:01:07,975
The rule from the top said no shelterers.

20
00:01:07,975 --> 00:01:10,230
The crowd on the stairs said otherwise.

21
00:01:10,930 --> 00:01:12,959
Within weeks, the government gave in,

22
00:01:12,959 --> 00:01:15,810
and the stations were
officially opened as shelters.

23
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:18,915
On the night of September twenty-seventh,

24
00:01:18,915 --> 00:01:22,435
one hundred seventy-seven thousand
people slept underground.

25
00:01:22,435 --> 00:01:24,962
But now the Tube had a
strange new problem:

26
00:01:24,962 --> 00:01:27,430
where do you put a small city every night?

27
00:01:28,130 --> 00:01:29,730
The first answer was paint.

28
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:32,489
Staff painted white lines
along the platforms,

29
00:01:32,489 --> 00:01:34,767
because the trains were still running.

30
00:01:34,767 --> 00:01:36,984
Until half past seven in the evening,

31
00:01:36,984 --> 00:01:40,160
shelterers had to stay eight
feet back from the edge.

32
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:44,963
After eight o'clock, they could move
up to just four feet from the trains.

33
00:01:44,963 --> 00:01:47,538
Around half past ten,
the last trains stopped,

34
00:01:47,538 --> 00:01:50,840
the lights were dimmed, and the
power in the rails was cut.

35
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:54,900
Then people slept wherever
they could: on platforms,

36
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:58,777
on stairs, in passages, even on
escalators that had stopped.

37
00:01:58,777 --> 00:01:59,994
Nobody had pillows.

38
00:01:59,994 --> 00:02:03,580
Most people had a coat, a
blanket and the cold concrete.

39
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:05,775
The best spots went fast.

40
00:02:05,775 --> 00:02:10,020
Some families queued from ten in the
morning to claim a patch of floor.

41
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:13,539
Soon, a black market
appeared: a good place on a

42
00:02:13,539 --> 00:02:16,710
platform could be sold for
two shillings and sixpence.

43
00:02:17,410 --> 00:02:20,594
So the authorities brought
in a new kind of ticket:

44
00:02:20,594 --> 00:02:22,655
not for travel, but for sleeping.

45
00:02:22,655 --> 00:02:24,999
Regular families got a reserved place.

46
00:02:24,999 --> 00:02:29,010
About one spot in ten stayed free
for strangers caught in a raid.

47
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:32,587
One short branch line, to
Aldwych, was closed to

48
00:02:32,587 --> 00:02:35,465
trains completely and
handed over to shelterers.

49
00:02:35,465 --> 00:02:40,330
People could now sleep on the tracks
themselves, because no train would ever come.

50
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:44,165
Then came the bunks:
twenty-four thousand of them,

51
00:02:44,165 --> 00:02:46,610
fixed along platform walls and tunnels.

52
00:02:47,310 --> 00:02:51,521
Laid end to end, those bunks would
have stretched for twenty-seven miles.

53
00:02:51,521 --> 00:02:53,483
So sleep was solved, more or less.

54
00:02:53,483 --> 00:02:57,290
But more than a hundred thousand
tired people still needed to eat.

55
00:02:57,990 --> 00:03:01,374
At first, families brought
their own food: sandwiches,

56
00:03:01,374 --> 00:03:04,069
cold tea in a flask,
maybe a slice of cake.

57
00:03:04,069 --> 00:03:06,388
And Britain was already on rationing,

58
00:03:06,388 --> 00:03:10,210
so every slice of bacon and every
spoon of sugar was counted.

59
00:03:10,910 --> 00:03:14,632
So London Transport turned its
own trains into delivery vans.

60
00:03:14,632 --> 00:03:17,286
They were called Tube
Refreshment Specials,

61
00:03:17,286 --> 00:03:19,940
and they left their
depots every afternoon.

62
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:23,762
By December nineteen forty,
one report said these

63
00:03:23,762 --> 00:03:26,692
trains carried seven tons
of food every night.

64
00:03:26,692 --> 00:03:31,420
That meant around thirty thousand buns,
plus endless cups of tea and cocoa.

65
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,680
By the end of the war,
London Transport had

66
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:37,180
served over half a million gallons of tea.

67
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:40,749
Staff and volunteers
served it from more than a

68
00:03:40,749 --> 00:03:43,862
hundred and thirty canteen
points on the platforms.

69
00:03:43,862 --> 00:03:46,340
But food goes in, and it has to come out.

70
00:03:46,340 --> 00:03:49,240
And that was the problem
nobody had planned for.

71
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:52,777
Tube stations were built
for passengers who stayed a

72
00:03:52,777 --> 00:03:55,560
few minutes, not for families
who stayed all night.

73
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:59,311
At Holborn, a station that
could hold four thousand

74
00:03:59,311 --> 00:04:02,483
six hundred people, there
were four chemical toilets.

75
00:04:02,483 --> 00:04:03,490
And four buckets.

76
00:04:04,190 --> 00:04:07,495
The air grew hot, heavy and
smelly, and doctors feared

77
00:04:07,495 --> 00:04:10,617
that one sick person could
infect a whole platform.

78
00:04:10,617 --> 00:04:12,800
Lice spread from blanket to blanket.

79
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:18,217
And in the warm tunnels, shelterers
complained about another enemy: mosquitoes.

80
00:04:18,217 --> 00:04:21,718
By early nineteen forty-one,
staff were spraying stations,

81
00:04:21,718 --> 00:04:23,830
careful to stop while people slept.

82
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:27,457
Slowly, things got better,
with proper toilets,

83
00:04:27,457 --> 00:04:30,883
first aid posts and nurses
on duty at the big shelters.

84
00:04:30,883 --> 00:04:33,748
But if the Tube was
crowded, dirty and smelly,

85
00:04:33,748 --> 00:04:37,050
why did families keep coming
back, night after night?

86
00:04:37,750 --> 00:04:39,779
Because down there, it was quieter.

87
00:04:39,779 --> 00:04:44,010
The bombs above sounded like distant
thuds instead of screaming whistles.

88
00:04:44,710 --> 00:04:49,186
At the peak, around twenty-five thousand
children spent the night in the stations.

89
00:04:49,186 --> 00:04:50,953
They played, did their homework,

90
00:04:50,953 --> 00:04:53,990
and slept between strangers
who soon became neighbours.

91
00:04:54,690 --> 00:04:59,851
Some shelters got lending libraries, concerts,
film shows and even evening lectures.

92
00:04:59,851 --> 00:05:04,890
For many families, the platform stopped
being a shelter and became a kind of home.

93
00:05:05,590 --> 00:05:09,356
Picture a typical evening for a
mother in the East End of London.

94
00:05:09,356 --> 00:05:11,754
She finishes work, collects the children,

95
00:05:11,754 --> 00:05:15,730
grabs the blankets and the shelter
ticket, and walks to the station.

96
00:05:15,730 --> 00:05:19,960
In the morning, she climbs back up to
see if her house is still standing.

97
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:23,281
Over the whole war,
London Transport counted

98
00:05:23,281 --> 00:05:26,080
about sixty-three million
visits by shelterers.

99
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:28,442
Underground, people felt safe.

100
00:05:28,442 --> 00:05:31,820
But they were about to learn
that the Tube was not bombproof.

101
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:35,632
The first warning came in
September, when a bomb hit

102
00:05:35,632 --> 00:05:38,683
the subway at Marble Arch
and killed twenty people.

103
00:05:38,683 --> 00:05:42,180
In October, Trafalgar Square
and Bounds Green were hit too.

104
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:45,219
On October fourteenth, nineteen forty,

105
00:05:45,219 --> 00:05:48,112
a heavy bomb hit the road
above Balham station.

106
00:05:48,112 --> 00:05:52,370
The crater was so big that a
double-decker bus drove straight into it.

107
00:05:53,070 --> 00:05:55,708
Water mains and sewers
burst, and the tunnel

108
00:05:55,708 --> 00:05:58,225
below began to fill with water and gravel.

109
00:05:58,225 --> 00:06:00,323
Sixty-eight people died that night,

110
00:06:00,323 --> 00:06:03,440
most of them shelterers,
four of them railway staff.

111
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:06,604
Three months later, a
bomb fell through the

112
00:06:06,604 --> 00:06:09,068
road onto the booking
hall at Bank station.

113
00:06:09,068 --> 00:06:11,072
More than fifty people were killed,

114
00:06:11,072 --> 00:06:14,620
and the crater was so large the
army built a bridge across it.

115
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:17,622
So the government finally ordered new,

116
00:06:17,622 --> 00:06:20,955
deeper shelters, dug far below
the existing Tube lines.

117
00:06:20,955 --> 00:06:22,633
Eight of them were finished.

118
00:06:22,633 --> 00:06:26,350
But they were not ready until
the worst of the Blitz was over.

119
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:30,108
So for those fifty-seven
nights, Londoners trusted

120
00:06:30,108 --> 00:06:32,861
old railway tunnels,
painted lines, and luck.

121
00:06:32,861 --> 00:06:37,400
Which leaves the real question: how
much did the Tube actually save London?

122
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:40,592
In November nineteen
forty, officials counted

123
00:06:40,592 --> 00:06:43,360
where Londoners were really
spending their nights.

124
00:06:44,060 --> 00:06:45,573
The result surprised them.

125
00:06:45,573 --> 00:06:48,600
Only about four percent were
sheltering in the Tube.

126
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:51,667
Nine percent used big public shelters,

127
00:06:51,667 --> 00:06:55,218
and twenty-seven percent slept
in small shelters at home.

128
00:06:55,218 --> 00:07:00,150
The rest, more than half of London, simply
stayed in their own houses and hoped.

129
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:03,726
So the Tube did not save
London by being big.

130
00:07:03,726 --> 00:07:06,858
It saved the people inside
it by being organised.

131
00:07:06,858 --> 00:07:09,185
Tickets, painted lines, food trains,

132
00:07:09,185 --> 00:07:12,610
and staff who kept a hundred
thousand strangers calm.

133
00:07:13,310 --> 00:07:17,838
The proof came in March nineteen
forty-three, at Bethnal Green station.

134
00:07:17,838 --> 00:07:21,124
During an alert, a new
anti-aircraft rocket battery

135
00:07:21,124 --> 00:07:24,280
nearby fired, with a sound
few people recognised.

136
00:07:24,980 --> 00:07:29,215
Crowds hurried down the dark stairs,
and a woman near the bottom fell.

137
00:07:29,215 --> 00:07:31,998
In seconds, people piled
on top of each other.

138
00:07:31,998 --> 00:07:35,930
One hundred seventy-three died,
and no bomb ever hit the station.

139
00:07:36,630 --> 00:07:38,835
That was the real lesson of the Tube.

140
00:07:38,835 --> 00:07:41,815
Underground, the biggest
danger was not the bombs.

141
00:07:41,815 --> 00:07:42,590
It was panic.

142
00:07:43,290 --> 00:07:48,052
The government kept the Bethnal Green
report quiet until the war was nearly over.

143
00:07:48,052 --> 00:07:51,607
And on the sixth of May, nineteen
forty-five, the last three

144
00:07:51,607 --> 00:07:55,400
hundred forty-four shelterers
climbed back up into the daylight.

145
00:07:56,100 --> 00:08:00,652
So how did Londoners eat, sleep and
survive fifty-seven nights underground?

146
00:08:00,652 --> 00:08:04,111
With a cheap ticket, a painted
line and tea from a train.

147
00:08:04,111 --> 00:08:06,660
And above all, by staying calm in a crowd.

148
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:10,407
But what about the people
who never went underground?

149
00:08:10,407 --> 00:08:12,792
Next, we open the tin shelter in the back

150
00:08:12,792 --> 00:08:15,467
garden that millions
trusted with their lives.

151
00:08:15,467 --> 00:08:17,250
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
